 of what it could possibly  do in terms of safety but it cost the  city you know three million dollars plus  just for the contract then there's a  bout of almost a million dollars in the  cost of the police for their time and  efforts to sit behind a desk and click  on mouse and and get people tickets all  day long and go to port and then and  then in terms of the revenue that comes  back from the tickets you know it's a  it's almost a $500 ticket here in less  Angeles in the state of California and  not very much of that goes to the city  the state takes a very large chunk of  that and the and then the county takes  some and then the courts take some so  all told the city gets probably less  than a hundred and fifty bucks or so per  ticket in most cases if there were  traffic cop there you probably wouldn't  see a ticket issued in the first place  it's rolling right turns that's correct  yeah the rolling right turn is 75  percent of the tickets and the citations  that are given we did a study because  there's has been this argument as you  know is a rolling right turn dangerous  and we did a study on that and we looked  at the database from the California  Highway Patrol that has all the  accidents in the state of California and  we looked at Los Angeles specifically  and we found that it's extremely rare  that an accident will occur for a  rolling right turn so what they're doing  is seventy-five percent of this  multi-million dollar program is going to  try to change a behavior that doesn't  actually cause any accidents it's more  or less a judgment call to I mean this  is a California as a right turn on red  state as my thinking probably most  states are at this point and it you know  this is the issue is it's just turned  and you never really come to a complete  stop you're already halfway through you  make the turn I did it twice on the way  to work another thing you found in in in  your studies and then I think there was  a 2008 study to back us up was that  